The clue literally said it wanted the name in Russian. How does the phonetic English spelling satisfy that requirement? Also, when the NYT prints most names from other languages, it just loses the diacritical marks and the like. This is how Iga Świątek becomes Swiatek. Sure, the English alphabet does not have those exact letters. Now explain to me why a different logic should apply to Пётр? When Americans at the NYT type Świątek, they don't phonetically transliterate her name. They just lose the diacritics (and naturally that also happens when Świątek features in the grid as an entry).

That SACHA from an old puzzle clue liteally called for a *Slavic* name - not for a Western equivalent. That made SACHA incorrect: an English or French transliteration is not a Slavic name.

@Andrzej

While I don’t feel the crossword puzzle creators or editors are intentionally misrepresenting other languages, it can be a sticking point.

I have another nit to pick when it comes to language inaccuracies:  to describe anything in ASL by only the hand shape is ridiculous.  Any hand shape is used for countless signs.

